WEBVTT

00:00:00.965 --> 00:00:04.366
NARRATOR: In 1493, Columbus
led a small band of men

00:00:04.366 --> 00:00:10.065
and 24 horses ashore on the
Caribbean island of Hispaniola.

00:00:10.065 --> 00:00:13.899
The island people, the Arawaks,
had seen white-skinned men

00:00:13.899 --> 00:00:15.799
on Columbus’s first voyage,

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:19.399
but they had never before laid
eyes on the towering beasts

00:00:19.399 --> 00:00:23.233
that splashed through the surf that day.

00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:25.633
As soon as Columbus
arrived in the Americas,

00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:28.632
worlds that had been
separated by vast oceans

00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:31.633
for millions of years began to merge.

00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:34.533
This process would transform life

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.199
on both sides of the Atlantic forever.

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:42.700
Sheep, cattle and chickens were
all imported into the Americas,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.199
as were bananas, coffee and wheat.

00:00:45.199 --> 00:00:50.133
And the Americas introduced
the world to tomatoes, cocoa,

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:52.266
as well as corn and peanuts-

00:00:52.266 --> 00:00:55.500
foods that now literally feed the world.
